# Serving

Two things are served: the static site on GitHub Pages, and the misconnect calculator's API on Vercel with its check log in Neon Postgres. The site never needs the API to tell the story; only the planner page writes.

{{ statement() }}

## The API

FastAPI in `packages/api`, run as a Vercel container function on the free Hobby plan and built from `Dockerfile.vercel`. It scales to zero when idle and starts on the first request, and the migration runs at every start. Its tables live in the `turnaround` schema of a Neon database. Live at {{ v('deploy.base_url') }} ({{ v('deploy.status') }}).

The API ran on Fly first. Fly now asks for a card on every operation, so it moved; DECISIONS.md has the entry.

| Route | What it does |
|---|---|
| `GET /v1/health` | the database round trip and the marts the calculator reads |
| `GET /v1/hubs` | the hubs the calculator answers for |
| `GET /v1/curve` | one connection's misconnect probability at every buffer, read only |
| `POST /v1/checks` | computes a check, writes the check row, then the audit row, then commits, then returns the id |
| `GET /v1/checks`, `GET /v1/checks/{id}` | the log, and one check with its score if it has one |
| `POST /v1/score` | scores every stored check whose travel month is the outcome month the marts hold |
| `GET /v1/scorecard`, `GET /v1/audit` | how the scored checks did; the audit trail |

Writes need the token as a bearer token in the `Authorization` header; without it a write is refused with HTTP 401 and nothing is stored. This is a demonstration, not a product: the token keeps the log clean, it does not protect anything valuable. Every response body carries the statement, errors included, and `tests/api/test_statement_on_every_endpoint.py` checks every route.

A check stores an origin, a connecting airport, a destination, a travel month, two scheduled hours, a buffer and the answer. It never stores a person. A note on a check is limited to lowercase words so it cannot carry a name, an email or a booking reference.

## How a check is answered

From the calculator marts the chapters stage writes (`results/marts/misconnect_*.parquet`, copied into the image at build time). For the connecting hub, the travel month and the two scheduled hours: the joint distribution of inbound arrival delay and outbound departure delay, in five minute cells; shifted by the route's own monthly delay where the route has enough flights; the probability that the inbound lands too late for the buffer less a minimum connection of {{ v('policy.min_connection') }}, or never lands. The interval is a Wilson interval widened by the design effect, because flights on the same day at the same hub are not independent. Every answer says how many flights it rests on, and a connection with too few is refused rather than guessed.

The latest month in the marts is held out of the cells and kept as the outcome month, so `POST /v1/score` grades the calculator on flights it never saw.

## Persistence, tested three ways

- `tests/api/test_check_log.py::test_check_is_committed` and `test_score_is_committed` read the rows back through a second connection opened independently of the app's pool.
- `test_audit_precedes_response` asserts the audit row is in the database before the response leaves.
- `scripts/check_persistence.py` starts the API in a separate process, posts a check, scores it against a fixture, and reads the rows back through a fresh connection; CI runs it on every push.

## Deploying

Vercel builds and serves the API. The setup is done once, in this order:

- Import the GitHub repository as a new Vercel project.
- Pick the Container preset and point it at `Dockerfile.vercel`. That file is `Dockerfile.api` listening on the port Vercel sends the traffic to.
- In the project settings, set `DATABASE_URL`, `TURNAROUND_WRITE_TOKEN`, `TURNAROUND_DB_SCHEMA` and `TURNAROUND_CORS_ORIGINS`.

After that, every push to `main` redeploys. There is no deploy script to run.

The checks run from a Windows machine with the repository and a `.env` beside it, through Git GUI's tools menu or PowerShell. They read the live address from `deploy/live-url.txt` and the write token from `deploy/write-token.txt`, so put the Vercel address and the token from the project settings there first.

    deploy\verify.ps1      # a check from a separate client, read back; writes results\deploy\verification.json
    deploy\replay.ps1      # the demo queue: checks at the busiest hubs so the log is never empty
    deploy\load-test.ps1   # writes results\latency\live.json
    deploy\reset.ps1       # deletes the rows the recordings and checks created

The function's logs are in the Vercel dashboard. `fly.toml`, `deploy\deploy.ps1`, `deploy/deploy.sh` and `deploy\logs.ps1` are the former Fly route, kept for history.

Secrets live in `.env` and in the Vercel project's environment variables, nowhere else. `deploy/live-url.txt` and `deploy/write-token.txt` are ignored by git.

